Z||000016||grinthock||11-08-2001||09:43 PM||||You do'nt need too, A relative of mine who drives a 2001 volvo transport says you don't even need to in the trucks anymore but it provides smoother shifts in the truck so he usually doubles if he's shifting with a big load.<P>Us?  Forget it.||||reg||1
Z||000017||tgowens||11-08-2001||09:50 PM||||Isn't "double-clutching" like typing the message and then hitting spell check and <I>then</I> hitting submit? I do that all the time, and I have an automatic!||||reg||1
Z||000018||filthyscarecrow||11-08-2001||10:42 PM||||i think what the import shop guy was talking about to fierotrevor is accelerating, then, when you shift, clutch in, shift to next gear, full throttle THEN slip the clutch all over again for the next gear. kinda giving it a CVT effect for a few seconds.<BR>1. it's not double clutching. double clutchin means just that- clutching twice. you're only clutching once there... <BR>2. kiss your clutchplates goodbye. not only is excessive heat generated that will result in more slip at higher RPM, but it can destroy a racing clutch. they aren't made to take that kind of abuse. racing clutches are built for mild starts, maybe a couple race starts then they get replaced. once the car is moving, the driver should not be slipping the clutch AT ALL. if yuo're slippping it that much and even if it does hold up, it will get so hot is will keep slippping, and you won't be able to put any more power down anyway. slip=slow (except in rallying).||||reg||1
Z||000019||Slammed Fiero||11-08-2001||10:44 PM||||Oh good lord! how the fast and the Furious has warped some peoples mind.<P>Still a pretty good movie , but lot of people came away from that even more confused than they already were.<P>Double clutching, is used on downshifting , for example into a corner where you don't want to upset the balance of a car by shifting into a lower gear and having it rev the motor wayyyyy up ..thus upsetting the chassis.<P>Head into corner ..Clutch in , shift to lower gear , rev motor to match rpm on next lowest gear you are shifting to. Do it right and you will hear the shift and not feel it.<P>You don't need to do it unless your on a track or driving really agressively. <P>As for thinking that shifting without a clutch is cool , and your some kinda bad ass from fast and furious ..your not ..your wearing out your tranny and you look like a dork. It's not a transport , it's a Fiero.<BR>I have a friend who drives a transport and thinks he's cool when he does it in his Corolla..just dumb<P>Thats my 2 cents about double clutching from a few courses at Powell motorsports.<P>Oh yeah ..not the best thing to rev up a Fiero and drop it in D or 1 either.. or any auto for that matter.<P>Favorite Quotes<P>"No Shi* a 2JZ engine" wow every supra has one<P>"He's got a Direct port NOs system...2 seconds later..he has a NOS fogger system" <P>Wanna learn something pick up a book.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||11-09-2001||12:00 AM||||For those who may not know what a syncro is or how it works.....<P>The stick article in my cave goes over one kind. <A HREF="http://www.howstuffworks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.howstuffworks.com</A>  explains another and even has it animated. I think How Stuff Works also explains double clutching.<P>If you have to double clutch a syncronized transmission, something is seriously wrong.<P>A syncro tranny can be shifted w/o clutch if you are Very careful and precise. Doing that wrong will beat up the syncros and the splines. Good way to trash a tranny quick. Some trannys tolerate this better than others.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
